In the builds and distributions (not in this repository)
=========================================================

The page (every build, desktop and Android)
--------------------------------------------
Angular (@angular/*, MIT), RxJS (Apache-2.0), tslib (0BSD). The Angular build writes their licence texts to
licences/3rdpartylicenses.txt, which the help page links.

The server's libraries (the desktop jar and the Android app)
------------------------------------------------------------
Kotlin, kotlinx (coroutines, serialization, io, datetime), Ktor and their dependencies (Apache-2.0); Typesafe
Config, kaml, okio, SnakeYAML Engine KMP, UrlEncoder (Apache-2.0); SLF4J (MIT); Logback (EPL-2.0 or LGPL-2.1,
desktop only). Maven's license plugin writes the exact list at every build to licences/THIRD-PARTY.txt, in the
desktop jar and in the Android app, which the help page shows. This NOTICE and the Apache License's text (LICENSE)
are in both too, served as licences/NOTICE.txt and licences/LICENSE.txt and linked by the help page; the Android app also
keeps the libraries' own licence files (META-INF/LICENSE*, NOTICE*).

The desktop distributions (releases/*-linux.tar.gz, *-windows.zip)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Listed with their versions in desktop/src/main/resources/licences/embedded.json, which the help page shows, and
downloaded by scripts/linux/setup.sh from the URLs externals.conf names:
- Eclipse Temurin JRE (OpenJDK), trimmed by jlink: GPL-2.0 with the Classpath Exception.
- Piper, piper-phonemize, ONNX Runtime, and {fmt}, spdlog and libtashkeel's model within them: MIT. eSpeak NG (its
  library and data, which Piper uses): GPL-3.0-or-later. Bundled unmodified, run as a separate program. Their
  licence texts, which Piper's own archive leaves out, are in piper/licences/ (from desktop/src/dist/piper-licences/),
  with where eSpeak NG's complete source code is.
- The Piper voices desktop/src/dist/files/VOICES.md lists, each with its own licence (CC-BY-4.0, CC-BY-SA-4.0 or public
  domain) and the attribution it asks for. Four of them (all but Cori) were fine-tuned from Piper's lessac voice,
  trained on the Lessac Blizzard 2013 data, licensed for research purposes only: VOICES.md says which.


